WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.255
Shell scripts are not all interpreted by
the same shell.

2
00:00:04.335 --> 00:00:09.527
A script written for Bash may fail or
behave differently under POSIX sh, dash,

3
00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:11.445
ksh, zsh, or another shell.

4
00:00:11.525 --> 00:00:16.877
The shebang at the top of a script is an
important signal because it tells readers

5
00:00:16.957 --> 00:00:21.511
and execution environments which
interpreter the script expects.

6
00:00:21.591 --> 00:00:25.186
When the code and the declared interpreter
disagree,

7
00:00:25.266 --> 00:00:27.902
portability problems appear quickly.

8
00:00:27.982 --> 00:00:33.255
ShellCheck can help identify constructs
that do not match the declared shell.

9
00:00:33.335 --> 00:00:38.927
For example, a script that claims to be
POSIX shell but uses Bash-specific features

10
00:00:39.007 --> 00:00:44.359
is likely to surprise someone when it runs
in a minimal container image or a CI/CD

11
00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:47.075
runner with a different default shell.

12
00:00:47.155 --> 00:00:49.153
The opposite can also happen:

13
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:53.067
a script may be written carefully for
broad portability,

14
00:00:53.147 --> 00:00:58.420
but its invocation path may accidentally
run it under a shell the author did not

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:58.899
test.

16
00:00:58.979 --> 00:01:03.053
Portability does not mean every script
must run everywhere.

17
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:07.927
Sometimes the right answer is to require
Bash and declare that clearly.

18
00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:13.519
Sometimes the right answer is to use
portable POSIX syntax because the script must

19
00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:15.276
run across many systems.

20
00:01:15.356 --> 00:01:17.673
The important point is intention.

21
00:01:17.753 --> 00:01:22.786
The team should choose the shell
deliberately, document the expectation,

22
00:01:22.866 --> 00:01:27.659
and test in the runtime environment where
the script will actually run.

23
00:01:27.739 --> 00:01:31.574
When ShellCheck reports dialect or
portability issues,

24
00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:36.527
reviewers should decide whether to change
the code, change the shebang,

25
00:01:36.607 --> 00:01:39.163
or change the execution environment.

26
00:01:39.243 --> 00:01:42.758
The fix is not always to make the script
more generic.

27
00:01:42.838 --> 00:01:47.552
The fix is to make the script,
declaration, and runtime match reality.

28
00:01:47.632 --> 00:01:53.464
Clear interpreter expectations reduce
surprises when scripts move between laptops,

29
00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:56.100
servers, containers, and pipelines.
